18. Why Docker?

The API can be run directly using Python and req.txt, but Docker provides an isolated and reproducible runtime environment.

Without Docker

Python
  ↓
pip install -r req.txt
  ↓
FastAPI
  ↓
Application

With Docker

Docker Container
 ├── Python 3.11
 ├── Required packages
 ├── ML model
 ├── FastAPI application
 └── Uvicorn

This makes it easier to move the application between development, testing, and deployment environments.

19. Input Validation

The project uses Pydantic schemas to validate incoming API data before it reaches the machine learning model.

The schema is maintained in:

schema/user_input.py

This provides a structured contract between the API client and the prediction service.

20. Error Handling and Production Considerations

For production deployment, the following should be considered:

Validate all incoming inputs

Handle invalid categorical values

Add proper exception handling

Avoid exposing internal errors

Keep secrets outside source code

Add logging

Add API health checks

Pin dependency versions

Monitor model performance

Keep model artifacts versioned

Use HTTPS behind a production reverse proxy

Avoid storing unnecessary files in the Docker image

21. .gitignore

The project ignores development-specific files such as:

.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.env
.idea/

This keeps unnecessary IDE, cache, environment, and secret files out of the Git repository.

